--- body ---
## Context
The specification chain has no terminology section. A reader entering
`spec/DESIGN.md` at §2.5 meets "conductor", "slabs", "the ledger" and
"stage keys" with nowhere to look them up.

0036 required that "every term of art [be] defined where it first appears",
and its rewrite delivered that: `census` is glossed at DESIGN.md §2.1,
the four stage keys at §2.2, the entry layer at §2.3. That is the right
first move and it is not sufficient, for the reason 0036 could not address:
a gloss at first appearance serves a reader going top to bottom, and nobody
reads a specification that way twice. Tickets, reviews and forge threads
enter the documents in the middle.

Raid 51's Imagine pass (2026-08-29) re-checked this ticket's premise and
narrowed it. The original Context named `graft`, `kill 9` and
`corpus-critic M4` as undefined jargon a reader would meet. They are not:
0036's rewrite purged all three, and they now appear nowhere in the tree
except inside 0036 (which cites them as jargon it removed) and this
ticket, which copied them. So this ticket is NOT rescuing undefined
jargon — that job is done. It is consolidating already-good definitions
into one alphabetical entry point for the mid-document reader. Executing
against the old example list would send an agent hunting terms that do
not exist.

The author's ruling (2026-08-29): a standalone document, not a section
inside one of the three.

Standalone also settles an ownership question the chain would otherwise
have. A term used by REQUIREMENTS.md, CONSTRAINTS.md and DESIGN.md alike
belongs to none of them; putting the glossary in any one would either
duplicate definitions or make two documents depend on a third's internals.
This is the one-statement-per-fact rule applied to vocabulary.

## Relevant files
- `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md` — new. The ticket was filed at 09:26 on 2026-08-29
  saying "at the repository root"; the `spec/` reorganisation landed at
  15:01 the same day (commit `6ae3fe8`), so "root" meant "beside the chain"
  and now resolves to `spec/`. Sibling ticket 0221 (PR #42) already assumes
  this path.
- `bench/check_governance.py` — add `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md` to `SCANNED`.
  Ticket 0221 names this ticket as its live trigger: a new specification
  document is unscanned by default, and unscanned is indistinguishable
  from having nothing to hide. This is the one-line stopgap, NOT 0221's
  fix (which inverts the default); 0221 stays open.
- `bench/check_terminology.py`, `tests/test_check_terminology.py`,
  `Makefile` — the new guard and its gate.
- `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md`, `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md`, `spec/DESIGN.md` — inline
  glosses that become redundant may collapse to the term alone; a first-use
  gloss that carries argument stays. Prefer leaving them: the figure guard
  anchors on sentences, and churn here is not what this ticket buys.
- `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md` — document-set list and table.

## Actions
1. Harvest the vocabulary from the chain (`spec/REQUIREMENTS.md`,
   `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md`, `spec/DESIGN.md`). Open tickets are a spot-check
   only, not an independent harvest pass: the Imagine sweep found no live
   ticket vocabulary that does not trace back to the chain. Roughly 25–30
   entries, in three marked buckets:
   - **ours** — ledger, stage keys (record/extract/chunk/embed), entries,
     slabs, passages, census, watermark, reconcile tick, conductor/P0/P1,
     custody string, quarantine, segmenter (seg/1), band 0 / band 1,
     sideline-never-delete, probe-don't-fix, first-with-text, fraction-RRF
     / entry collapse, the locator, the four gates, coverage, metadata-only,
     prefix in R2/R26's sense.
   - **inherited, Zotero** — item, attachment, library / `libraryVersion`,
     `Last-Modified-Version`, the `?since=` version cursor, the `/fulltext`
     census endpoint, `Zotero-Server-ID`, local API, `dateAdded`.
   - **inherited, SQLite/FTS5** — FTS5, unicode61, bm25, WAL. In scope:
     each appears in the chain (FTS5 four times in DESIGN.md, five in
     CONSTRAINTS.md) and a newcomer cannot tell which words this project
     coined. Marking the bucket is the point.
   Excluded, deliberately: "the wall", which lives only in `STATE.md` —
   outside the chain per CLAUDE.md's document-set list, and outside this
   ticket's own verification criterion, which is scoped to DESIGN.md.
2. Write each entry as: the term, one-sentence definition, and where it is
   authoritative. Definitions POINT at the owning document for anything
   numeric; the glossary owns no thresholds.
3. Mark deprecated and panel-era terms explicitly as historical, with the
   git coordinate. Three terms — `graft`, `kill 9`, `corpus-critic M4` —
   plus `v1` / "The Settled Ledger", all sharing one coordinate:
   `panel/cycle2/` at commit `e32afe3`. A short subsection, not a research
   project.
4. Register the document in CLAUDE.md and README.md, and add it to
   `SCANNED` in `bench/check_governance.py`.

## Test
`bench/check_terminology.py` + `tests/test_check_terminology.py`, following
the `check_governance.py` precedent in shape (`run(root)` returning an exit
code, fixture repositories under `tmp_path`) but INVERTING two of its
choices, deliberately:

- **Default-deny, not default-allow.** `check_governance.py` hunts named
  bound-phrases because it scans thousands of lines of legitimate design
  prose where digits are the norm. TERMINOLOGY.md is one small hand-authored
  file where every digit was deliberately typed, so the safe rule is: any
  digit in a definition fails, unless it matches a narrow allowlist — git
  SHA, ISO date, `[RCDX]\d+` reference code, `§` section mark, version
  string (`v1.9.0`, `Zotero 10`, `SQLite 3.43`), ticket ID.
- **A pointer does NOT excuse a number.** This is the opposite semantic from
  `test_pointer_on_the_line_excuses_it`. This ticket's invariant is "the
  glossary owns no thresholds", so `"refreshes every 60 s (DESIGN.md §2.4)"`
  must still fail — the number is restated regardless of the citation.
  Copying the governance guard verbatim would silently weaken the invariant.

Red first, against this seeded entry:

    - **census** — a full listing of every item, refetched every 60 s.
      Authoritative: DESIGN.md §2.4.

Required tests: the positive control above; a clean fixture passing; each
allowlist class admitted individually (so none is dead vocabulary); and a
missing-file test — if `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md` is absent the guard must fail
loudly, never report "0 entries, 0 violations, clean".

Known and documented limitation, stated in the guard's docstring the way
`check_figures.py` states its own: a threshold restated in words
("roughly every minute") carries no digit and passes. Regex cannot close
that; `verify-adherence` and human review cover it. A guard that guessed
at prose numbers would produce noise.

## Verification
- [x] Every term in the glossary resolves to an owning document
- [x] No design number is restated in a definition
- [ ] A reader can enter DESIGN.md at a random section and look up every
      unfamiliar term
- [x] `make check` green, including the new `terminology` target

## Invariants
- The glossary defines; it never decides. A definition that would settle an
  open design question goes to DECISIONS.md first.
- One statement per fact: no threshold, budget or rule is duplicated here.
- The figure guard's 160 pairs stay green: if an inline gloss is collapsed,
  check `bench/check_figures.py` still anchors.

## Exit criteria
- [x] `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md` exists, covering the chain's vocabulary and the
      inherited Zotero and SQLite/FTS5 vocabulary, each entry marked ours
      or inherited
- [x] Historical and panel-era terms marked as such with a git coordinate
- [x] Registered in CLAUDE.md and README.md
- [x] Added to `SCANNED` in `bench/check_governance.py` so it does not
      arrive unguarded (0221's trigger case, closed for this document only)
- [x] The no-numbers guard is in place, red-first proven, and `make check`
      passes
